This is a first-order linear differential equation of the form dy/dx + P(x)y = Q(x) with P(x) = 2 and Q(x) = e^x.
The integrating factor is e^∫P(x)dx = e^∫2dx = e^{2x}.
Multiplying both sides by e^{2x}:
e^{2x}dy/dx + 2e^{2x}y = e^{2x}e^x = e^{3x}
The left side is d/dx(e^{2x}y), so:
d/dx(e^{2x}y) = e^{3x}
Integrating both sides: e^{2x}y = ∫e^{3x}dx = (1/3)e^{3x} + C
y = (1/3)e^x + Ce^{-2x}
The general solution is y = (1/3)e^x + Ce^{-2x}, where C is an arbitrary constant.
